  <p class="post-meta post-meta--light"><span class="badge">Guides</span><time datetime="2026-10-01">1 October 2026</time><span>2 min read</span></p>
  <h1>How to scope a software project so the estimate means something</h1>
  <p class="lead">A practical way to describe a software project so a development partner can give you an estimate you can rely on.</p>
</div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container article-layout">
  <article class="prose article" data-article>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px; border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0,0,0,0.06); border: 1px solid var(--border); background: #0f172a;">
      <img src="{{ asset('assets/img/blogs/how-to-scope-software-project.svg') }}" alt="How to scope a software project so the estimate means something" style="width: 100%; max-height: 440px; object-fit: cover; display: block;">
    </div>
    <p>Most disappointing software projects share one root cause: the scope was never really agreed. A budget and a deadline were set, but nobody wrote down what &quot;done&quot; meant. Here is a simple way to avoid that.</p>
<h2 id="start-with-the-problem-not-the-features">Start with the problem, not the features</h2>
<p>Write two or three sentences describing the problem the software must solve and who has it. &quot;Our dispatcher spends two hours a day phoning drivers for status&quot; is far more useful than &quot;we need an app with GPS.&quot;</p>
<h2 id="describe-the-users-and-what-they-must-be-able-to-do">Describe the users and what they must be able to do</h2>
<p>List each type of user and the five or six most important things they need to do. This turns a vague idea into a set of testable outcomes, which is the foundation of any honest estimate.</p>
<h2 id="separate-must-haves-from-nice-to-haves">Separate must-haves from nice-to-haves</h2>
<p>Be ruthless. A first release should contain only what is needed to deliver the core value. Everything else goes on a backlog. Teams that skip this step usually find the budget gone before the important features are finished.</p>
<h2 id="list-the-systems-it-must-connect-to">List the systems it must connect to</h2>
<p>Payment gateways, accounting tools, messaging services and existing databases all add work. Naming them early avoids unpleasant surprises later.</p>
<h2 id="be-open-about-constraints">Be open about constraints</h2>
<p>Fixed launch dates, hosting requirements, compliance needs and available budget all shape the right approach. A good partner would rather know than guess.</p>
<h2 id="ask-for-a-range-and-the-assumptions-behind-it">Ask for a range and the assumptions behind it</h2>
<p>For anything non-trivial, an estimate is a range with assumptions. Ask what would make it go up or down. If a partner will only give you a single confident number without asking questions, treat that with caution.</p>
<h2 id="phase-the-work">Phase the work</h2>
<p>Splitting a project into phases reduces risk. You learn from real use after the first release and can adjust the plan before spending the rest of the budget.</p>
<p>If you would like help turning an idea into a clear brief, <a href="/start-a-project">start a project enquiry</a> and describe what you have so far. A rough brief is enough to begin a conversation.</p></article>
